# bb-contents Core Conventions

## Project Overview

`@bebranded/bb-contents` is a vanilla JS IIFE library for Webflow. It adds features via `data-bb-*` HTML attributes. Single file, no external dependencies, loaded via CDN or npm. Version must always be in sync across 3 places (see Publishing).

## Module Pattern

Every module lives in `bbContents.modules` and follows this exact contract:

```js
moduleName: {
    detect: function(scope) {
        // Return true if the page has elements for this module
        return (scope || document).querySelector(bbContents._attrSelector('bb-attr')) !== null;
    },
    init: function(root) {
        const scope = root || document;
        if (scope.closest && scope.closest('[data-bb-disable]')) return;
        const elements = scope.querySelectorAll(bbContents._attrSelector('bb-attr'));
        elements.forEach(function(element) {
            if (element.bbProcessed) return; // always guard re-init
            element.bbProcessed = true;
            // ... module logic
        });
    }
}
```

- Always use `bbContents._attrSelector('bb-name')` for queries (dual `bb-*` + `data-bb-*` support).
- Always use `bbContents._getAttr(element, 'bb-name')` to read attribute values.
- Always guard with `element.bbProcessed` to prevent double-initialization.
- Respect `[data-bb-disable]` at the top of `init`.

## Security

Before any DOM insertion:
- Text content → `bbContents.utils.sanitize(value)`
- CSS values → `bbContents.utils.escapeCss(value)`
- URLs → validate with `bbContents.utils.isValidUrl(url)` first
- Country codes → validate with `bbContents.utils.isValidCountryCode(code)`

## Error Handling

Never leave an empty or silent `catch`. Always at minimum `console.error`:

```js
// ❌ BAD
} catch (e) {}

// ✅ GOOD
} catch (e) {
    console.error('[bb-contents] Module failed:', e);
}
```

Retry loops must always have a hard max (e.g. `_maxInitRetries`). Never use infinite polling without a timeout.

## Documentation

**Every code change must be reflected in documentation.**

### JSDoc inside `bb-contents.js`

Each module must have a JSDoc comment block above it describing its purpose and all supported attributes:

```js
/**
 * Module Tooltip
 * @attr {string} bb-tooltip - Tooltip text to display
 * @attr {'top'|'bottom'|'left'|'right'} [bb-tooltip-position=top] - Position
 */
tooltipName: {
```

### `README.md`

`README.md` is the public-facing reference. Keep it up to date whenever:
- A new module is added → add a section with its name, description, and all `data-bb-*` attributes
- A module attribute is added, changed, or removed → update the relevant section
- Installation instructions change → update the installation section

The README structure to maintain per module:

```markdown
## Module Name

Brief description.

| Attribute | Values | Default | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `data-bb-attr` | — | — | Main trigger |
| `data-bb-attr-option` | `a` \| `b` | `a` | Option description |
```

### When to update

| Action | JSDoc | README |
|---|---|---|
| Add a module | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |
| Add an attribute | ✅ Required | ✅ Required |
| Change behavior | ✅ Update | ✅ Update if user-facing |
| Bug fix (no API change) | — | — |

## Publishing Workflow

**Always publish GitHub + npm simultaneously. Version must be identical in all 3 places:**
1. `package.json` → `"version"`
2. `bb-contents.js` → `BB_CONTENTS_VERSION` constant (line ~13)
3. `bb-contents.js` → `@version` JSDoc header (line ~4)

| Target | GitHub | npm |
|---|---|---|
| `develop` / test | `git push origin develop` | `npm publish --tag test` |
| `main` / live | `git push origin main` | `npm publish` (tag `latest`) |
